sources read:
  samples/fixtures/token_breakdown.jsonl

attribution coverage: 6 of 6 assistant turns were usage-bearing; 0 carried no recognised usage field
usage-field presence across usage-bearing turns (a field missing here is a silent 0, not a measured one):
  input_tokens: 6/6
  output_tokens: 6/6
  cache_creation_input_tokens: 6/6
  cache_read_input_tokens: 6/6
sidechain (subagent) turns: 2

TOTAL over 6 usage-bearing turns: in=49 out=490 cache_create=4900 cache_read=20500
cache_read : output overhead = 41.84

BY TOOL (turn-grain: a turn's usage is attributed to the SET of tools it called, not split per call):
  turns      out  cache_read cache_create  tools
      1      200        8000         2000  Bash+Grep
      1      100        5000         1000  Read
      1       40        3000          400  Grep
      2       70        2500          700  (no tool call)
      1       80        2000          800  Task

BY BASH COMMAND (Bash turns clustered by command's first token after stripping `cd ...&&` chains and `VAR=` prefixes; a heuristic, not a parse -- `git commit`/`git push` both cluster as `git`. Keys are a CLOSED vocabulary: a public command word, `(other)`, or `(unparsed)`):
  turns      out  cache_read cache_create  bash command cluster
      1      200        8000         2000  git

BY AGENT (same-session Task subagents separated from the main thread):
  turns      out  cache_read cache_create  agent
      4      430       16000         4300  main thread
      2       60        4500          600  explore

TOP 5 TURNS BY cache_read (the turns re-reading the most cached context):
   cache_read      out  agent / tools
         8000      200  main thread / Bash+Grep
         5000      100  main thread / Read
         3000       40  explore / Grep
         2000       80  main thread / Task
         1500       20  explore / (no tool call)

LOOP FLAGS
  Mechanical signals, computed from transcript STRUCTURE only -- turn boundaries and
  tool_use block counts -- never from judging what was said. THESE ARE FLAGS, NOT
  VERDICTS: a flagged span means an unusual amount of work by the threshold YOU set,
  which is a pointer to look. It is not a finding that the work was wasted, and no
  threshold here is a validated constant.

  real user turns (span boundaries): 1
  user entries NOT counted as operator input: none
    No isMeta, tool_result-only or engine-injected user entry was seen at all. For a
    real Claude Code transcript that is itself unusual -- tool results come back as user
    entries -- so check that this is the format you think it is.

  SIGNAL 1 -- tool calls per span between consecutive real user turns
    spans: 1  tool calls per span: p50=6 p90=6 max=6  (assistant turns per span: p50=6 max=6)
    threshold: NOT SET -> NOT COMPUTED
      Not a clean result and not a dirty one: no span was tested. No default ships
      because no published threshold is grounded -- the frameworks' own ceilings span 10
      to 1000 (each read at first hand; see docs/citations.md) and exist as cost
      backstops, not as non-convergence detectors. Pick one from the distribution above
      via --flag-span-tool-calls N or ARI_FLAG_SPAN_TOOL_CALLS, and treat it as a
      starting point for recalibration.

  SIGNAL 2 -- the same tool called with the same arguments, repeatedly
    tool calls: 6, distinct call signatures: 4
    longest consecutive run of one signature: 2
    most occurrences of one signature within a single span: 2
    3 repeat group(s) found (a signature is a tool plus a DIGEST of its arguments;
    arguments themselves are never printed):
      count  window      where                  signature
          2  consecutive main thread            Read#44136fa3
          2  span        span 1                 Read#44136fa3
          2  span        span 1                 Grep#44136fa3
    threshold: NOT SET -> NOT COMPUTED
      Nothing is flagged. The counts above are reported anyway, because they need no
      constant; what would be flagged does. Set one with --flag-repeated-calls N or
      ARI_FLAG_REPEATED_CALLS.
      Repeated invocations are counted MECHANICALLY: the identical tool and identical
      arguments, by digest. A repeat is not a finding that the work was wasted --
      polling until a condition holds, retrying a flaky step, and re-reading a file that
      changed all repeat legitimately, and this instrument cannot tell those from a
      stuck loop because that needs the state the transcript does not record. What it
      gives you is where to look. Matching is EXACT, which under-reports on purpose: a
      retry whose arguments differ by a timestamp or a page token does not match, and no
      normalisation is shipped because none of the obvious ones is grounded -- the same
      reason no threshold is shipped. So treat a count here as a floor.

  SIGNAL 3 -- consecutive assistant turns that called no tool
    longest streak anywhere: 1 turn(s)
    main thread: 4 turns, 1 with no tool call, in 1 streak(s), longest=1
    sidechains: 2 turns, 1 with no tool call, in 1 streak(s), longest=1
    threshold: NOT SET -> NOT COMPUTED
      No streak was tested. Same reason as signal 1 for shipping no default; set one
      with --flag-no-tool-streak N or ARI_FLAG_NO_TOOL_STREAK.
